Changelog: 04-11-2026 02:52:00

Added. run.ps1 starts Nexus Sound Lab on Windows with the checkout's Windows venv, as run.sh does on Linux (owner,
04-11-2026), so Nexus Dashboard's tile for it is on there too. Sound and the controller go through Qt and pygame
as on Linux; not tried on Windows yet.

Changelog: 03-11-2026 02:11:57

Added. Nexus Sound Lab, a Nexus app in dev/sounds/ (owner, 03-11-2026: a sound generator with sliders to make and
try short sound effects, grown from a click-sound demo for the tick a window plays when the controller moves the
focus). A preset (Tick, High tick, Blip, Thock, Click, Select, Back, Error, Tick after silence) and a waveform
(sine, triangle, square, saw, noise) to start from; a slider for each number of the sound: the pitch at the start
and the end of a tone, its length, attack and decay, the number of tones and the interval between them, the noise
over the tone and its decay, the silence before, and the volume (sound_lab/synth.py renders it from arithmetic
alone, 44.1 kHz 16-bit mono). The sound is drawn beside the sliders and plays when a slider is let go, with Play,
or when the focus moves between the buttons of the Try it row (Tab, the arrow keys or a controller's d-pad through
core's Gamepad; A plays it again). Save as WAV writes it for a suite's sounds folder. Keep the audio link awake
(on by default) plays a silent loop while the window is open: PipeWire pauses the output when nothing plays and an
HDMI TV mutes until the stream starts again, so a 50 ms tick was over before it was heard (found on the board,
03-11-2026); the info button explains it. The last sound, the preset, the choice and the save folder are
remembered in the settings. It runs from the checkout with the dev venv (run.sh, bc250_core from core/), takes
Nexus's design and icon (core's nexus.py), and is never released (.checkout-only); install.sh writes the launcher,
menu entry and Desktop icon for this checkout.
